# Every text file checks out with LF on every platform. Tests read committed
# files byte for byte and compare them with what the code writes:
# bench/src/baseline.rs include_str!s bench/baseline.json and asserts it is
# what format_baseline emits. Git on Windows defaults core.autocrlf to true,
# which rewrites LF to CRLF at checkout, so that assertion failed on all six
# Windows lanes of the monster matrix (#1013) while the file on main was
# correct. The rule is global, so the next byte-exact test needs no line
# here: a checkout artifact is never a test's concern.
* text=auto eol=lf

# tests/golden/ and tests/adversarial/ pin exact bytes: golden.rs decodes a
# committed frame and asserts it equals the committed plaintext byte for
# byte, and torture.rs/adversarial.rs feed committed bytes straight to the
# decoder. Git's line-ending conversion must never touch either directory,
# on any platform, regardless of whether a given fixture's content looks
# like text (issue #518: Windows checkout rewrote a golden .plaintext
# fixture's LF line endings to CRLF, so the decoded frame no longer matched
# the pinned plaintext -- a checkout artifact, not a decode regression).
# `-text` is stronger than the global rule above: it also keeps a seed that
# happens to carry a CR (tests/adversarial/random-garbage) out of checkin
# normalization.
tests/golden/** -text
tests/adversarial/** -text
